\description{
Confidence interval on the end-point of a stratigraphic (or sighting) range
that does not assume randomly placed finds, only that gap sizes come from a
continuous distribution with no trend along the section. The \code{C}
confidence interval equals the \code{C}th percentile of the gap-size
distribution (Marshall, 1994, p. 460), which is bracketed by order
statistics of the \code{N} observed gaps. With \code{F(x)} the binomial
\code{Binomial(N, C)} distribution function and \code{gamma = (1 - conf_prob) / 2}:
the lower limit on the interval length is the \code{(x + 1)}th smallest gap,
\code{x} the largest integer \verb{< N} with \code{F(x) <= gamma} (equation 1); the upper
limit is the \code{(x + 1)}th smallest gap, \code{x} the smallest integer with
\code{F(x) >= 1 - gamma} (equation 2). With probability \code{conf_prob} (the paper's
confidence probability \verb{1 - 2 gamma}) the length of the \code{C} interval lies
between the two. If no \code{x < N} satisfies equation 1 there is no lower
limit; if only \code{x = N} satisfies equation 2 there is no upper limit and the
interval is unbounded (e.g. a 95\% interval needs at least 72 gaps at
\code{conf_prob = 0.95}).
